1
00:00:05,828 --> 00:00:15,723
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, the sutta for today, it's Anguttara Nikaya 539, that we have translated as Cannot Endure.

2
00:00:18,029 --> 00:00:28,759
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it starts by the Buddha addressing the bhikkhus and giving the comparison with the bull elephant that is not worthy and worthy of a king.

3
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:36,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, the sutta is not very very long or complex, but it does raise some interesting points about the whole

4
00:00:37,828 --> 00:00:42,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Nature of samādhi and where the samādhi comes from, to put it that way.

5
00:00:42,971 --> 00:00:49,176
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the examples the Buddha gave, he starts by saying how the royal bull elephant cannot endure sights.

6
00:00:51,418 --> 00:01:02,866
[SPEAKER_00]: And it is when a royal bull elephant gone to the battle falters and founders at the sight of a division of elephants, of cavalry, of chariots, of infantry.

7
00:01:03,066 --> 00:01:05,949
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't stay firm and fails to plunge into a battle.

8
00:01:06,927 --> 00:01:10,168
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how a royal bull elephant cannot endure sights.

9
00:01:11,668 --> 00:01:14,349
[SPEAKER_00]: How can that royal bull elephant not endure sounds?

10
00:01:15,069 --> 00:01:15,869
[SPEAKER_00]: And then goes the same.

11
00:01:15,929 --> 00:01:25,051
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when it's gone to the battle, it flinches, falters at the sound of division of elephants, the thunder of drums, the cattle drums, the horns.

12
00:01:25,111 --> 00:01:26,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, it gets scared.

13
00:01:26,311 --> 00:01:27,251
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't stay firm.

14
00:01:28,032 --> 00:01:30,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Fails to plunge into the battle.

15
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:36,872
[SPEAKER_00]: How is the royal bull elephant... how can the royal bull elephant not endure smells?

16
00:01:37,853 --> 00:01:47,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Says it is when a royal bull elephant gone to the battle falters fallen founders when it smells the odor of the feces, urine of the battle-hardened pedigree royal bull elephants.

17
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:49,380
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't stay firm.

18
00:01:50,721 --> 00:01:51,962
[SPEAKER_00]: How can it not endure tastes?

19
00:01:52,998 --> 00:01:53,818
[SPEAKER_00]: And it goes the same.

20
00:01:53,959 --> 00:02:04,004
[SPEAKER_00]: When it's basically during the battle, it misses a meal, grass, water, misses two, three, four, five meals, it doesn't stay firm, gives in, the mind gets overwhelmed.

21
00:02:05,905 --> 00:02:08,026
[SPEAKER_00]: How is the royal bull elephant cannot endure touches?

22
00:02:08,246 --> 00:02:14,549
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when he's prodded by the arrows, spears, gets hit, cannot stay firm.

23
00:02:14,869 --> 00:02:18,051
[SPEAKER_00]: So it cannot remain unmoved.

24
00:02:19,492 --> 00:02:32,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So, elephant that has not been trained in remaining unmoved in those five ways, is the elephant that's not worthy of a king, not worthy of a... well, not worthy to go into battle.

25
00:02:34,227 --> 00:02:45,751
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same way, a bhikkhu with five qualities is not worried of offerings dedicated to the gods, not worried of hospitality, not worried of religious donation, not worried of veneration with joint palms, and it's not the supreme field of merit for the world.

26
00:02:47,512 --> 00:02:48,052
[SPEAKER_00]: What five?

27
00:02:48,372 --> 00:02:53,494
[SPEAKER_00]: When a bhikkhu cannot endure sight, sound, smell, taste and touches.

28
00:02:53,774 --> 00:03:00,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, when it comes to the bhikkhu, the sutta says that bhikkhu cannot endure sights,

29
00:03:01,585 --> 00:03:06,792
[SPEAKER_00]: When seeing a sight with their eyes, he's aroused by a desirable sight.

30
00:03:07,673 --> 00:03:11,979
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not able to compose the mind as a result of that.

31
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:14,363
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how bhikkhu cannot endure sights.

32
00:03:16,493 --> 00:03:18,434
[SPEAKER_00]: How is it that a bhikkhu cannot endure sounds?

33
00:03:19,154 --> 00:03:24,156
[SPEAKER_00]: It is when a bhikkhu, hearing a sound with their ears, is aroused by a desirable sound.

34
00:03:25,197 --> 00:03:26,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, he cannot compose the mind.

35
00:03:27,998 --> 00:03:33,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the lack of composure is the result of that arousal, pretty much.

36
00:03:34,161 --> 00:03:36,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes for the smells.

37
00:03:36,662 --> 00:03:42,104
[SPEAKER_00]: When smelling an odor with the nose is aroused by a desirable smell, he's not able to compose the mind.

38
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,761
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point here, as some of you noticed,

39
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:26,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when I sent that email out, is that when it came to the comparison with the elephant, the whole thing was about enduring battle, discomfort, fear,

40
00:04:28,387 --> 00:04:33,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Threat faltering and flinching in face of all of those prospects.

41
00:04:34,270 --> 00:04:40,413
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, when it came to the comparisons with... for the bhikkhus, it talks about pleasures.

42
00:04:41,113 --> 00:04:43,814
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't talk about bhikkhus enduring pains and so on.

43
00:04:44,915 --> 00:04:55,160
[SPEAKER_00]: And... that's because the entire training of the mind, training of the elephant, training of the battle,

44
00:04:56,138 --> 00:04:59,441
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the level of where the desires are.

45
00:05:00,361 --> 00:05:07,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, generally, when an unpleasant feeling touches you, you might have a desire to get rid of it.

46
00:05:08,027 --> 00:05:15,753
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have a desire against it, but that desire is not as deeply rooted, to put it that way, as an essential desire.

47
00:05:16,453 --> 00:05:26,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a desire that will be pertaining to a lot kind of more, say, existential prospects of engaging with the world on account of your desire.

48
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:32,903
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's also something that, even for some mundane reasons, you can convince yourself to endure.

49
00:05:33,464 --> 00:05:39,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you find a good reason, you will endure hardship, you will endure hunger, you will endure discomfort.

50
00:05:39,827 --> 00:05:43,690
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's hard to find a good reason to endure saying no to your desires.

51
00:05:45,185 --> 00:05:55,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not accidental that this is not mentioned because, more or less, as I said, pretty much anybody can muster up determination or a sense of

52
00:05:56,569 --> 00:06:07,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody will, if motivation to to put up with ascetic practice or with this or with that, but nobody will accidentally be practicing sense restraint.

53
00:06:07,752 --> 00:06:13,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody will accidentally choose to stick to saying no to their to their senses repeatedly and so on.

54
00:06:13,794 --> 00:06:21,677
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the battleground and that whole thing revolves pretty much on the level of where your desires are, where that pressure is felt.

55
00:06:22,597 --> 00:06:26,218
[SPEAKER_00]: But, so, the point is that if somebody is not trained

56
00:06:27,975 --> 00:06:31,438
[SPEAKER_00]: And during these five, there is no samādhi.

57
00:06:32,198 --> 00:06:33,439
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no mental composure.

58
00:06:35,121 --> 00:06:56,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, you know, it doesn't mean you should you should not try to be composed, but you have to recognize that if you are still driven and taking for granted the desire that comes, that pressures you on account of sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, you are disqualifying yourself from the establishment of samādhi.

59
00:06:57,933 --> 00:07:11,445
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not an accident that the whole training of an unfaltering mind, a non-flinching mind, comes through training in regard to desire, regarding sight, sound, smell, taste and touches.

60
00:07:12,706 --> 00:07:22,274
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sutta obviously gives this comparison, then continues and says, okay, so how is then the royal bull elephant worthy of the king?

61
00:07:22,574 --> 00:07:25,017
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, well, it's worthy because he can endure the sights,

62
00:07:26,723 --> 00:07:31,306
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't falter at the sights of an infantry, division of elephants, the battle.

63
00:07:31,746 --> 00:07:33,947
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't get scared.

64
00:07:33,987 --> 00:07:38,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes for sounds, same goes for smells, same goes for tastes, touches.

65
00:07:39,330 --> 00:07:41,591
[SPEAKER_00]: And such elephant is worthy of a king.

66
00:07:44,207 --> 00:08:04,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, in the same way, a bhikkhu is worthy of offerings if he trains in regard to sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, but, again, not trains blindly through, like, I don't know, ascetic practice of avoiding sights, avoiding sounds, avoiding tastes, eating only once every six weeks or something, because that's the training.

67
00:08:04,535 --> 00:08:04,835
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

68
00:08:05,195 --> 00:08:12,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Training in regard to desire, the prospect of desire that arises on account of a taste, on account of a sight,

69
00:08:14,593 --> 00:08:19,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not as easy as just... just avoid... avoid sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

70
00:08:20,336 --> 00:08:29,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, in the beginning, you have to start simply through withdrawing yourself truly, like, overwhelming amount of sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

71
00:08:30,868 --> 00:08:34,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So that you can train in regard to the desire that would be provoked by those.

72
00:08:35,250 --> 00:08:43,935
[SPEAKER_00]: And same, if we stick to the example of the elephant, you would not be immediately throwing a wild, untamed, untrained elephant into the battle.

73
00:08:44,115 --> 00:08:47,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, obviously, it will run away and it won't fight.

74
00:08:49,578 --> 00:08:59,508
[SPEAKER_00]: But sometimes people kind of confuse or take too far the recognition that, okay, so I do have to avoid sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches to some extent.

75
00:08:59,988 --> 00:09:02,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I will adopt a view that those must be avoided.

76
00:09:03,011 --> 00:09:09,218
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more I avoid them, the better, the closer to nibbāna or some sort of freedom from craving I'm getting.

77
00:09:09,258 --> 00:09:10,239
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not correct.

78
00:09:11,144 --> 00:09:19,090
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to avoid it, relatively speaking, avoid the obvious unwholesome ones, that they simply have no practical value or any benefit whatsoever.

79
00:09:20,871 --> 00:09:25,215
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you actually have to be training the mind in regard to those, not just be saying no to them.

80
00:09:26,365 --> 00:09:28,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's kind of the main gist of the sutta.

81
00:09:29,126 --> 00:09:35,129
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I did ask what constitutes enduring of sights, sounds, smells, tastes and touches.

82
00:09:35,750 --> 00:09:40,872
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not enduring these battlegrounds, to put it that way.

83
00:09:40,892 --> 00:09:55,320
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not enduring by just blindly saying no or trying to deny the fact that your eyes see and get attracted to sights, your ears hear and prefer sounds, your nose likes smells and so on.

84
00:09:56,960 --> 00:10:02,104
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, to be able to contain them, you need to be able to not act out of them.

85
00:10:03,085 --> 00:10:06,808
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when I keep talking about enduring things on the right level, that's what the right level is.

86
00:10:07,188 --> 00:10:11,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, enduring things on the right level means containing things on the right level.

87
00:10:14,133 --> 00:10:15,114
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you contain them,

88
00:10:17,297 --> 00:10:19,918
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where that whole training develops.

89
00:10:20,018 --> 00:10:26,099
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, how would you train an elephant that's not used to the battle drums, the smell of feces?

90
00:10:26,159 --> 00:10:36,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by exposing it to it in a controlled manner, so that it gets used to, so that it can overcome its inherent tendency to flinch and look away and run away.

91
00:10:37,002 --> 00:10:41,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the next thing I asked was like, why is the sixth sense, the mind, omitted?

92
00:10:41,923 --> 00:10:45,604
[SPEAKER_00]: The first reason is because by training the five senses, you are training the mind.

93
00:10:45,984 --> 00:10:46,964
[SPEAKER_00]: You are developing that.

94
00:10:47,564 --> 00:11:04,773
[SPEAKER_00]: But the second reason is, you will not be able to, say, not act out of the delight towards phenomena, towards images, towards ideas, if you haven't been already sufficiently trained in regard to sight, sound, smell, taste and touches.

95
00:11:06,215 --> 00:11:14,945
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, all those talks when we talk about not allowing things the center stage, they do kind of pertain to that later stage of development.

96
00:11:15,886 --> 00:11:18,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, you should be considering it and aiming for it.

97
00:11:19,449 --> 00:11:23,314
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact is that unless

98
00:11:24,845 --> 00:11:30,128
[SPEAKER_00]: You are an elephant that doesn't flinch in face of sight, sound, smell, taste and touches.

99
00:11:30,749 --> 00:11:33,290
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no training in regard to the images in the mind.

100
00:11:33,330 --> 00:11:34,611
[SPEAKER_00]: It's simply you're not seeing it.

101
00:11:35,312 --> 00:11:50,722
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order for that to be really, like, clearly outlined, defined, you know, in the in the peripheral and so on, that mind and images, this training, the training of the battleground, needs to be cultivated.

102
00:11:51,541 --> 00:11:52,662
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as it takes.

103
00:11:55,443 --> 00:12:02,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that kind of leads to the next question, which is like, how does the battleground simile translate to your everyday life?

104
00:12:03,048 --> 00:12:04,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is the battleground?

105
00:12:04,548 --> 00:12:05,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Where do you look for the battleground?

106
00:12:05,989 --> 00:12:10,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you just go and start saying no to random things to experience the battleground?

107
00:12:10,852 --> 00:12:13,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you sort of need to fabricate it in your mind?

108
00:12:14,074 --> 00:12:17,916
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think by now, obviously, the answer is relatively obvious.

109
00:12:17,976 --> 00:12:19,577
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like where your desires are.

110
00:12:20,951 --> 00:12:21,712
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's another thing.

111
00:12:21,753 --> 00:12:24,417
[SPEAKER_00]: See, there's that sutta that...

112
00:12:24,458 --> 00:12:31,430
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't remember which one, but there's a layman talking to the Buddha and he said that

113
00:12:33,037 --> 00:12:55,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he was kind of impressed with the Buddhist teaching because he said, like, when he or they, as householders, hear about giving up sensuality, giving up desires, giving up the engagement with the senses, that feels, for him, like a sudden abyss, like a precipice.

114
00:12:55,987 --> 00:12:57,088
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that type of fear.

115
00:12:58,073 --> 00:13:04,460
[SPEAKER_00]: When you hear like, oh, you gotta give up the sensual pleasures of the world.

116
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:19,595
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that's another reason why the simile does not refer to the painful feelings that a monk has to endure, because they do not necessarily bring that, as I said, deeply existential kind of threat that a person used to sensuality would feel.

117
00:13:20,540 --> 00:13:25,641
[SPEAKER_00]: When they hear or find out or even try to say no to their desires in the beginning.

118
00:13:26,101 --> 00:13:38,703
[SPEAKER_00]: And this layman was telling to the Buddha, like, oh, it's amazing that, you know, your teaching and the monks seem to be enjoying doing it, because when I hear about giving sensuality, it just feels like I'm just at the edge of a massive abyss, a precipice.

119
00:13:39,244 --> 00:13:43,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you get, like, full-on that anxiety and panic.

120
00:13:44,525 --> 00:13:46,465
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where the battleground is.

121
00:13:47,543 --> 00:14:01,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Battleground is on the level of saying no to your sense desires, keeping the lid on your actions, and you're going to experience, you're going to start experiencing anxiety, doubt, mental pressure.

122
00:14:01,431 --> 00:14:07,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Elephant will try to make you, you know, let it run away, allow it to go away.

123
00:14:08,994 --> 00:14:10,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's really where the training is.

124
00:14:11,315 --> 00:14:15,856
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not even so much about the sight sounds, sounds of the drums and so on.

125
00:14:15,976 --> 00:14:19,777
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about what they invoke in the mind that's not trained.

126
00:14:20,237 --> 00:14:28,899
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can imagine, imagine like, you know, well, through the course of our rebirth, we were all at some point fighting in some war, somewhere.

127
00:14:29,439 --> 00:14:33,700
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to imagine, say, you were with some small army,

128
00:14:34,720 --> 00:14:35,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Sitting on top of some...

129
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:45,983
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know... in some valley, in some... on some land somewhere, and you're just surrounded by the sounds of enemies' drums, coming from the mountains, coming from everywhere else.

130
00:14:47,364 --> 00:14:54,326
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like... you're gonna feel afraid, because, see, there's lots of implications in those sounds now, coming your way.

131
00:14:55,786 --> 00:14:57,787
[SPEAKER_00]: And... but, see, it would be easier then.

132
00:14:58,860 --> 00:15:09,626
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'm sure that what happens in wars and these battles, how they used to be fought, it would be a lot easier to then just charge or run away.

133
00:15:10,127 --> 00:15:11,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, okay, if you can run away, you run away.

134
00:15:11,848 --> 00:15:22,394
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can't run away, it would be easier to charge in the battle, even if it's the tenfold army that's waiting for you, than to stay there and listen to the drums.

135
00:15:23,755 --> 00:15:24,415
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

136
00:15:26,168 --> 00:15:27,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's unpleasant.

137
00:15:27,409 --> 00:15:30,331
[SPEAKER_00]: That discomfort comes from inside, comes from that abyss.

138
00:15:31,811 --> 00:15:39,596
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like... And, as I said, I'm pretty sure that's what happens in wars when... when somebody gets too much on that psychological level, they just charge.

139
00:15:39,876 --> 00:15:41,777
[SPEAKER_00]: They just charge with a knife against the...

140
00:15:42,498 --> 00:15:44,139
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know... guns and so on.

141
00:15:44,599 --> 00:15:46,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's easier than to stay on that level.

142
00:15:47,461 --> 00:15:49,722
[SPEAKER_00]: You know you're gonna die, but at least it will end.

143
00:15:50,102 --> 00:15:51,643
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's what you think at a time.

144
00:15:52,324 --> 00:15:53,364
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the same point.

145
00:15:53,404 --> 00:15:55,466
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you start practicing the sense restraint,

146
00:15:56,400 --> 00:16:03,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no to your sensual desires, say, that would have been uninhibited beforehand, or up to that point.

147
00:16:04,431 --> 00:16:12,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're gonna feel like your whole entire world now, that's around you, is the world of that threat of that desire.

148
00:16:14,667 --> 00:16:25,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if it started through, like, saying no to a specific thing, specific object of your desire, it's very quickly gonna become that all-encompassing abyss.

149
00:16:26,290 --> 00:16:27,090
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the training.

150
00:16:27,450 --> 00:16:28,090
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

151
00:16:28,110 --> 00:16:34,692
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the virtue comes first, so that when you get overwhelmed with doubt, with everything else, I don't know what to do, well, you know what not to do.

152
00:16:37,819 --> 00:16:47,108
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, staying on that level, even when you know that it's a training, it's something that you need to keep reminding yourself, because it will be easier.

153
00:16:47,729 --> 00:16:51,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if, say, if you're not giving in to sensuality, that would be the easiest.

154
00:16:51,673 --> 00:16:53,154
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the least resistance option.

155
00:16:53,575 --> 00:16:55,296
[SPEAKER_00]: But, say, you would have built up enough

156
00:16:56,537 --> 00:16:58,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Determination, value, faith in the teachings.

157
00:16:58,758 --> 00:16:59,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are not giving in.

158
00:16:59,878 --> 00:17:01,019
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not breaking the precepts.

159
00:17:01,439 --> 00:17:05,780
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, it does... another... there is a real risk to go then too far.

160
00:17:05,880 --> 00:17:09,341
[SPEAKER_00]: To be like the one who charges the... the... the... at the drums.

161
00:17:09,861 --> 00:17:10,562
[SPEAKER_00]: To... to end it.

162
00:17:10,662 --> 00:17:14,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't stay on that level where that anxiety and doubt persist.

163
00:17:15,723 --> 00:17:18,744
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it would be easier then to say double down in your efforts.

164
00:17:19,884 --> 00:17:21,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Say no to more things.

165
00:17:23,013 --> 00:17:24,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Withdraw yourself even more.

166
00:17:24,734 --> 00:17:26,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Concentrate even harder.

167
00:17:27,977 --> 00:17:28,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

168
00:17:29,017 --> 00:17:34,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the sound of the surrounding battle drums is driving you insane.

169
00:17:36,082 --> 00:17:44,428
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize the only true way to deal with it, the only true way for that elephant to be developed as well, is to stay with it.

170
00:17:44,709 --> 00:17:46,490
[SPEAKER_00]: And that would be the true, you know, warrior.

171
00:17:47,428 --> 00:18:11,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, whether you charge at the battle drums, or you run away from them, or, say, you choose to, I don't know, concentrate on your nostrils, or watch your sensations, until you forget where you are, hypnotize yourself, basically, into forgetting the drums, and then you're seemingly peaceful, and then you wake up, then you come out of that state, and then you still have to deal with the drums.

172
00:18:11,500 --> 00:18:17,123
[SPEAKER_00]: You still have to deal with the mind that flinches in face of the drums, because you haven't addressed it.

173
00:18:18,630 --> 00:18:35,520
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you actually persevere on that level, don't lose yourself in self-hypnotic practices, don't charge blindly to, like, doubling down in efforts and self-torment and so on, don't revert back to sensuality, the elephant will get used to.

174
00:18:35,700 --> 00:18:36,580
[SPEAKER_00]: It will not flinch.

175
00:18:37,421 --> 00:18:44,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if the drumming continues for three months, like, the elephant will be calm.

176
00:18:44,874 --> 00:18:53,462
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, whatever happens after that, whether there is an actual battle ensuing or no, you already dealt with it.

177
00:18:55,304 --> 00:18:59,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Through training your mind on that level, it has already addressed the battleground.

178
00:18:59,227 --> 00:19:01,489
[SPEAKER_00]: It has already addressed the battle and the enemy.

179
00:19:01,870 --> 00:19:06,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Whichever way the battle goes, it becomes secondary, becomes unimportant.

180
00:19:09,309 --> 00:19:11,130
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that kind of answers this question as well.

181
00:19:11,230 --> 00:19:17,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it still somewhat possible to compose your mind before training in the enduring of the five objects?

182
00:19:18,134 --> 00:19:29,940
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as I said, if by that composing you mean, you know, start investing effort in your precepts, in sense restraint, then, yes, it's possible to compose your mind.

183
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:34,282
[SPEAKER_00]: But if by compose your mind mean can you, through concentration practices,

184
00:19:35,357 --> 00:19:39,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Speed up the process of getting used to the frightening battle drums?

185
00:19:39,478 --> 00:19:40,258
[SPEAKER_00]: No, not a chance.

186
00:19:41,198 --> 00:19:42,238
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes you away from it.

187
00:19:43,198 --> 00:19:48,999
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you hypnotize yourself away, so it's gone, and when you come back, it's still waiting for you.

188
00:19:50,320 --> 00:19:59,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it never addresses the root issue, which is that the mind flinches, falters in face of the abyss, in face of saying no to sensuality.

189
00:20:01,241 --> 00:20:04,102
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, yeah, people have asked, like, so

190
00:20:05,451 --> 00:20:12,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is Bhikkhu not being worthy of offerings, if he doesn't train in this way?

191
00:20:15,018 --> 00:20:18,380
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess, again, by now, the answer should be obvious.

192
00:20:18,420 --> 00:20:25,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, if there is no training in regards to sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, well, there is no training in the dhamma.

193
00:20:27,206 --> 00:20:28,747
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the answer is simple.

194
00:20:29,087 --> 00:20:32,890
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not worthy of offerings, because he's not the one practicing the dhamma.

195
00:20:35,691 --> 00:20:42,354
[SPEAKER_00]: And, consequently, the one who is training in regard to the senses, is thus training in samādhi, is thus training in dhamma.

196
00:20:43,034 --> 00:20:44,654
[SPEAKER_00]: And, that's why he's worthy of offerings.

197
00:20:45,275 --> 00:20:51,837
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the unpleasantness of the senses is what cannot be endured, as the name of the sutta says.

198
00:20:54,018 --> 00:20:56,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And, in a way, yes.

199
00:20:56,598 --> 00:20:57,619
[SPEAKER_00]: But, then, in another way, no.

200
00:20:58,539 --> 00:21:03,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the unpleasantness of the senses is what kind of

201
00:21:04,053 --> 00:21:07,335
[SPEAKER_00]: What makes you look off your senses, is what makes you look past them.

202
00:21:09,756 --> 00:21:21,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're, as we spoke before in our, you know, in other talks, how that inherent patiga, that resistance towards your own sense bases, they're uncomfortable, they're burden, they're exposure.

203
00:21:22,402 --> 00:21:27,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, by default, underlying tendencies make you make you look past them.

204
00:21:28,376 --> 00:21:33,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, technically, what you cannot endure is the fear that arises on account of removing the axis to their counterparts.

205
00:21:48,612 --> 00:21:56,097
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, obviously, the unpleasantness, the inherent unpleasantness of the senses, is, sure, fueling that fear even more.

206
00:21:56,897 --> 00:22:10,526
[SPEAKER_00]: But, fundamentally, even if you are, say, trained, if the elephant is trained, and there is no more fear of faltering in regard to the sight, sound, smell, taste, touch as they try to trap you or pressure you, you would be feeling that unpleasantness.

207
00:22:12,038 --> 00:22:15,081
[SPEAKER_00]: of the... of your senses, for as long as the aggregates don't break apart.

208
00:22:15,921 --> 00:22:31,474
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... you don't even need to think about specifically having to endure it or something like that, because there is no more fear arising on account of not being able to escape the confinement of those senses through engaging with sense objects.

209
00:22:33,638 --> 00:22:38,844
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how is... the second question is, how is aroused by a desirable object to be understood?

210
00:22:39,124 --> 00:22:40,826
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, that's again a good question.

211
00:22:40,886 --> 00:22:43,568
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what constitutes arousal?

212
00:22:44,529 --> 00:22:49,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Does seeing agreeable things immediately mean desire is there?

213
00:22:50,876 --> 00:22:54,960
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you know where the line is?

214
00:22:54,980 --> 00:22:56,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you see something you like,

215
00:22:58,027 --> 00:23:01,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that mean, oh, so sight of that already means a desire?

216
00:23:02,050 --> 00:23:08,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is there some space, so to speak, in between, before it becomes a desire?

217
00:23:11,418 --> 00:23:12,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it depends.

218
00:23:13,580 --> 00:23:19,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, if your desire, if your behavior hasn't been checked, seeing it will immediately mean a desire.

219
00:23:20,831 --> 00:23:33,784
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you would have been practicing restraint, keeping the virtue, seeing the things of the agreeable nature, seeing, smelling, tasting, touching, will not immediately give rise to the mind of desire.

220
00:23:34,325 --> 00:23:35,926
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will be able to see that difference.

221
00:23:36,106 --> 00:23:39,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Through practicing of the sense restraint, that's pretty much the purpose of it.

222
00:23:39,986 --> 00:23:56,060
[SPEAKER_00]: ...is to highlight and pretty much expose a difference between... yeah, that's... which is factually agreeable, factually preferable, and then the thirst, the desire, the pressure that overtakes the mind.

223
00:23:57,881 --> 00:24:06,649
[SPEAKER_00]: So, aroused by a desirable object refers to the mind being overtaken towards its thirst, towards sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

224
00:24:08,877 --> 00:24:24,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reason why this is important to distinguish is because, again, you could easily fall into, as I said, that doubling down effort and just saying no twice as much because you implicitly are blaming sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches for your desires, for the arousal.

225
00:24:25,991 --> 00:24:30,875
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you keep doing that, you will keep overlooking the level where the problem is.

226
00:24:33,277 --> 00:24:40,187
[SPEAKER_00]: And, is the underlying tendency of fear displayed in the elephant, somewhat present in the bhikkhu as well, when facing sense objects?

227
00:24:41,369 --> 00:24:42,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, certainly, in the beginning.

228
00:24:42,791 --> 00:24:43,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Like...

229
00:24:45,125 --> 00:25:02,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear, anxiety, doubt... pretty much the five hindrances will be the five ways that an untrained mind will keep trying to get you to flinch, to falter, to run away from the the drums that are scaring you or run at them.

230
00:25:03,501 --> 00:25:05,743
[SPEAKER_00]: One way or the other, it wants to make you act.

231
00:25:06,443 --> 00:25:12,268
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is all always kind of... underneath all of that, it's always going to be that fear and doubt.

232
00:25:13,949 --> 00:25:19,833
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also, like, you could see where the power of faith would come in.

233
00:25:20,934 --> 00:25:28,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, because, you know, sometimes you can reason, sometimes you can remind yourself, you know, through the right mindfulness, that, well, this is staying on the right level.

234
00:25:28,940 --> 00:25:32,662
[SPEAKER_00]: But sometimes none of that will be heard by your own mind.

235
00:25:32,842 --> 00:25:36,325
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, just out of faith, you just have to stay at that level.

236
00:25:37,205 --> 00:25:41,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as frightening as it is, it's the safest place to be.

237
00:25:42,807 --> 00:25:45,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Running away will probably get you killed, because you're surrounded.

238
00:25:46,709 --> 00:25:49,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Running at it will certainly get you killed even faster.

239
00:25:49,930 --> 00:25:54,532
[SPEAKER_00]: So, neither running forward nor running backward will at least give you the most time.

240
00:25:55,952 --> 00:26:00,734
[SPEAKER_00]: But, that means you do have to stay on the level of that mental unease.

241
00:26:01,595 --> 00:26:07,697
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's far harder than, as I said, even just charging in the battle with a pocket knife or something.

